  "narrative": "The enrichment results are clear and the key pathway memberships are confirmed. I now have sufficient evidence to write the narrative.\n\n**Narrative**\n\nThe metabolomics data reveal a profound and statistically robust remodeling of **tryptophan metabolism** (KEGG map00380, FDR < 0.0001, fold-enrichment = 700.5) in the treatment group. Seven of the ten differentially-abundant metabolites — L-tryptophan (C00078), serotonin (C00780), melatonin (C01598), indoleacetaldehyde (C00637), tryptamine (C00398), 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid (C00632), and quinolinic acid (C03722) — map to this pathway, with an additional three (1-pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid, pyrroline hydroxycarboxylic acid, and L-valine) appearing in the broader \"Biochemical pathways: part I\" annotation, consistent with their biochemical relatedness to tryptophan intermediates. A second, highly specialized pathway — **tryptophan catabolism leading to NAD⁺ production** (WP4210, fold-enrichment = 939.8) — also reaches significance, implicating the kynurenine branch that funneling tryptophan toward nicotinamide cofactor biosynthesis is altered.\n\nWithin the **tryptophan–serotonin–melatonin axis**, directional KEGG reaction-graph analysis confirms that L-tryptophan is the metabolic precursor of serotonin via a single enzymatic step (through tryptamine/indoleacetaldehyde), and that serotonin is in turn the precursor of melatonin. Both downstream connections are confirmed traversable in the forward direction. Serotonin and melatonin are well-established as key regulators of mood, circadian rhythm, and neuroimmune signaling, and their coordinated alteration alongside the parent amino acid suggests either enhanced biosynthetic flux or altered catabolism of the pathway.\n\nThe presence of **quinolinic acid** (C03722) is particularly noteworthy. Quinolinic acid is a neuroactive intermediate of the kynurenine branch of tryptophan metabolism and is a direct precursor to NAD⁺. Its elevation, alongside 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid, indicates that the treatment drives tryptophan carbon toward the kynurenine pathway at the expense of (or concurrently with) the serotonin/melatonin branch. Quinolinic acid acts as an *N*-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor agonist and a source of oxidative stress in the brain; elevated levels have been linked to neuroinflammation, excitotoxicity, and neurodegenerative processes.\n\nThe key driver compounds are therefore **L-tryptophan** (the pathway entry point), **serotonin and melatonin** (downstream neuroeffectors, both verified downstream of tryptophan), and **quinolinic acid** (branch-point intermediate toward NAD⁺, implicated in neurotoxicity). The parallel elevation of metabolites in both the serotonin/melatonin branch and the kynurenine/quinolinic acid branch suggests that the treatment globally activates tryptophan catabolism, with potential consequences for neuroendocrine homeostasis and neuroinflammatory tone.",
